          "thread": "Tornado Cash developer prosecutions",
          "why": "The parallel trials of Pertsev in the Netherlands and Roman Storm in the US forced a single urgent question onto readers: does authoring open-source privacy software make you legally responsible for every transaction it later routes?"
        },
        {
          "source": "https://www.defieducationfund.org/u-s-v-storm-2026-update/",
          "thread": "Code-is-law vs criminal liability",
          "why": "The Eisenberg Mango Markets case and the Peraire-Bueno MEV exploit trial both centered on defendants who argued they followed the rules the code set — readers engaged because the verdict would determine whether that defense holds in a US courtroom."
        },
        {
          "source": "https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/samuel-bankman-fried-sentenced-25-years-his-orchestration-multiple-fraudulent-schemes",
          "thread": "Crypto fraud executive accountability",
          "why": "Back-to-back trials of SBF, Do Kwon, and Eisenberg gave readers a running scoreboard on whether the architects of the 2022 collapse cycle would actually face prison time."
        },
        {
          "source": "https://abcnews.com/US/judge-denies-new-trial-sam-bankman-fried-after/story?id=132471994",
          "thread": "Insider cooperation and hidden backstops",
          "why": "Whistleblower testimony revealing Jump Trading's role in propping up UST and Gary Wang's cooperation in the FTX case pulled readers in because they exposed the off-chain mechanics that made on-chain collapses possible."
        },
        {
          "source": "",
          "thread": "Institutional protocol testnet pilots",
          "why": "Babylon's Bitcoin staking testnet, the ABN AMRO and 21X tokenized-asset Polygon trade, and Japan's Project Pax stablecoin trial represented the other meaning of 'trial' — real-money institutional experiments that signal where regulated capital is actually testing crypto rails."
        },
        {
          "source": "https://www.bankingdive.com/news/judge-tosses-krakens-major-questions-doctrines-defense/738368/",
          "thread": "Regulatory forum and jury-right precedents",
          "why": "The Supreme Court's ruling that SEC defendants are entitled to a jury trial rather than in-house tribunals, alongside the Ripple pre-trial motion, showed readers that the procedural battleground matters as much as the substantive charges."
        }
      ]
